## 2. Asset-general formulation

### 2.1 Notation

For an asset $a\in\{\text{USD/INR},\ \text{Brent},\ \text{Gold}\}$ with price $P^{a}_t>0$:

$$
r^{a}_t=\ln P^{a}_t-\ln P^{a}_{t-1},\qquad R^{a}_{t\to t+H}=\sum_{k=1}^{H} r^{a}_{t+k}=\ln\frac{P^{a}_{t+H}}{P^{a}_t}.
$$

Log-returns are additive over time and approximately equal to percentage changes for small moves; they are unit-free, so the same estimator applies to *INR per USD*, *USD per barrel* and *USD per troy ounce*.

### 2.2 What is asset-specific and what is not

| Component | Asset-agnostic? | Asset-specific consideration |
|---|---|---|
| Mode states $s_h$, elastic net, GARCH-$t$, simulation, CW/PIT/CRPS | Yes | Hyper-parameters (half-lives, $\nu$, persistence) will differ by asset |
| Drive set $\{x_j\}$ | **No** | For target = Brent, drop `brent_oil_r`; for target = Gold, drop `gold_r`. Otherwise $x_j(t)=r_t$ and the regressor duplicates the target's own contemporaneous return, producing exact collinearity with the fast memory modes (e.g. $s_{h=1}$ contains $r_t$) |
| Data construction | **No** | FX: spot/fixing and time-zone; Brent: roll-adjusted futures, not spot; Gold: spot or LBMA/futures fixing |
| Calendar | **No** | `pd.bdate_range` has no holiday calendar (§9) |
| Economic sign priors for $\Omega_j$ | **No** | See §5.4 |

### 2.3 Stylized facts the model class must accommodate (all three assets)

Fat tails, volatility clustering, near-zero return autocorrelation, leverage/asymmetry (strongest in equities, weaker in FX and gold; oil and gold can show *inverse* asymmetry), and regime shifts. See [Cont (2001)](https://doi.org/10.1080/713665670). The model captures the first two; asymmetry and regime shifts are outside it (§9–§10).

