## 4. Physical analysis (Mori–Zwanzig / GLE)

### 4.1 The statistical-physics template

For a resolved observable $A(t)$ of a larger deterministic system, projection-operator methods ([Zwanzig 1961](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.124.983); [Mori 1965](https://doi.org/10.1143/PTP.33.423)) yield the exact generalized Langevin equation

$$
\dot A(t)=i\Omega A(t)-\int_0^{t}K(t-s)A(s)\,ds+F(t),
$$

with $F$ evolving under the *orthogonal* dynamics and, for stationary equilibrium dynamics, the second fluctuation–dissipation relation $K(t)=\langle F(t)F(0)\rangle\langle A^2\rangle^{-1}$ ([Kubo 1966](https://doi.org/10.1088/0034-4885/29/1/306)). Optimal-prediction and data-driven applications: [Chorin, Hald & Kupferman 2000](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.97.7.2968); [Lei, Baker & Li 2016](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1609587113).

### 4.2 How the pipeline maps onto it

| Physical object | Pipeline object | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Resolved variable $A$ | $r_t$ (return) | Direct |
| Memory kernel $K(t-s)$ | $-K_k=-\sum_h\beta_h\lambda_h^{k}$ (sign convention aside) | Estimated, finite-parameter |
| Externally applied "fields" $x_j$ | Drives $x_j(t)$ | Linear response ([Kubo](https://doi.org/10.1088/0034-4885/29/1/306)) with coefficients $\Omega_j$ |
| Orthogonal-dynamics noise $F$ | Residual $\eta_t$ | **Not** orthogonal-dynamics noise; it is the regression residual with GARCH-$t$ structure |
| FDT-2 | Not imposed | Correctly disclaimed in the script |

### 4.3 Markovian embedding

A kernel of the form $\sum_h\beta_h e^{-\gamma_h \tau}$ is equivalent to coupling the resolved variable to auxiliary linear degrees of freedom $s_h$ relaxing at rates $\gamma_h$ — the classical *Markovian embedding* of a memory kernel (used for colored-noise GLE thermostats and for approximating long-memory kernels by exponential sums; e.g. [Abi Jaber & El Euch 2019](https://doi.org/10.1137/18M1170236) for rough-volatility kernels). The discrete $\lambda_h=e^{-\gamma_h\Delta t}$ with $\gamma_h=\ln2/h$ per day. The stacked system $S(t+1)=AS(t)$ of §3.2 is precisely this embedding in discrete time, and "stability of $A$" is the discrete analogue of positivity/decay of the embedded dynamics.

Related financial structures built on the same exponential-sum idea: Hawkes processes with exponential kernels ([Hardiman, Bercot & Bouchaud 2013](https://doi.org/10.1140/epjb/e2013-40107-3); [Bacry, Mastromatteo & Muzy 2015](https://doi.org/10.1142/S2382626615500057)), the HAR realized-volatility model whose daily/weekly/monthly averages are a coarse exponential family ([Corsi 2009](https://doi.org/10.1093/jjfinec/nbp001)), and propagator models for order-flow impact ([Bouchaud, Gefen, Potters & Wyart 2004](https://doi.org/10.1080/14697680400020325); [Lillo & Farmer 2004](https://doi.org/10.2202/1558-3708.1226)).

### 4.4 Dimensional and scaling remarks

$r$ is dimensionless; in continuous time $K(\tau)$ has units of 1/time, and the discrete tap is $K_k\approx K(k\Delta t)\Delta t$ (dimensionless per day). $\Omega_j$ carries units of (return)/(drive return) per day, i.e. dimensionless for log-return drives. Standardized coefficients are in return units per sd of the regressor.

### 4.5 Rigorous reading

* The Mori–Zwanzig derivation is exact for closed Hamiltonian systems with a known invariant measure; financial markets are open, non-Hamiltonian and non-stationary at long scales. The script's own caveats (no equilibrium, no FDT, no detailed balance, weak stationarity only) are therefore *necessary* for honesty, and they are present.
* The discrete model is equivalently an **ARX($\infty$)** with a parametric kernel and conditionally heteroskedastic errors. Its identifiability statement — total linear dependence cannot be split into "memory" and "noise autocorrelation" from one trajectory — is correct (spectral factorization is unique only up to the choice of minimum-phase representation).
* Because the same linear structure can be written as an ARMA-type filter, the choice between "memory $K$" and "serially correlated $\eta$" is a modelling convention, not an empirical fact.

