## 5. Financial and economic analysis

### 5.1 Efficient-markets baseline and what predictability can exist

Under weak-form efficiency returns are unpredictable in mean ([Fama 1970](https://doi.org/10.2307/2325486); modern perspective [Timmermann & Granger 2004](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0169-2070(03)00012-8)). Small, time-varying predictability can coexist with efficiency through risk premia, frictions and intervention. Economically small $R^2$ values can still matter for portfolio decisions ([Campbell & Thompson 2008](https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhm055)), but out-of-sample evidence is the standard ([Welch & Goyal 2008](https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhm014)).

**Implication for kernel signs.** $K_\Sigma>0$ = short-horizon momentum / time-series trend ([Moskowitz, Ooi & Pedersen 2012](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2011.11.003)); $K_\Sigma<0$ = reversal (liquidity provision, bid–ask bounce, official smoothing). Both are *total linear dependence* (§4.5).

### 5.2 The random-walk benchmark in FX and its modern status

[Meese & Rogoff (1983)](https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-1996(83)90017-X) showed structural FX models do not beat a random walk out of sample. Subsequent work refined, but did not overturn, the picture: [Rossi (2013)](https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.51.4.1063) surveys the evidence and its time-variation; [Engel & West (2005)](https://doi.org/10.1086/427467) explain why near-unit-root fundamentals make exchange rates look like random walks; [Rogoff & Stavrakeva (2008)](https://www.nber.org/papers/w14071) ✅ argue that reported gains were overstated through misinterpretation of nested-model tests, over-reliance on asymptotic statistics and insufficient window-robustness checks. **Each of those three criticisms maps onto a specific design feature of this pipeline** (non-asymptotic caution in the report text; window-robust *frozen-forward* and *nested chronological* validation; explicit nested-model handling). The multiple-testing exposure that remains is discussed in §6.6.

### 5.3 Volatility and density: where reproducible skill usually is

Volatility clustering is the most robust empirical regularity across FX, oil and gold ([Cont 2001](https://doi.org/10.1080/713665670)); GARCH(1,1) is a strong benchmark ([Hansen & Lunde 2005](https://doi.org/10.1002/jae.800)). For *distributional* forecasts, a conditional-variance model typically improves log score and CRPS against an unconditional benchmark even when the mean is unpredictable. Consequently:

> In `evaluate_crps_horizons`, the `zero_return` and `expanding_drift` benchmarks are **unconditionally homoskedastic** (empirical historical cumulative returns, centred), whereas the `drives_only` and `modes_plus_drives` candidates carry GARCH-$t$ conditional variance. A CRPS gain of a candidate over `zero_return` therefore conflates (i) conditional-mean skill and (ii) volatility-clustering skill. The cleanest *mean-skill* comparison is `drives_only` vs `modes_plus_drives` (both GARCH-$t$), which is how the script reports "gain from memory".

### 5.4 Asset-specific economic structure

| | **USD/INR (FX)** | **Brent crude** | **Gold** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Regime | De facto managed float; central-bank intervention and regime shifts documented ([Zeileis, Shah & Patnaik](https://research.wu.ac.at/en/publications/testing-monitoring-and-dating-structural-changes-in-maximum-likel-3/) ✅, R package [`fxregime`](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/fxregime/index.html) ✅; "fear of floating": [Calvo & Reinhart 2002](https://doi.org/10.1162/003355302760193886); classification: [Reinhart & Rogoff 2004](https://doi.org/10.1162/003355304772839515)) | Global benchmark set by supply/demand shocks and expectations ([Kilian 2009](https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.99.3.1053)); price-level shifts driven by OPEC/geopolitics | Store of value; driven by real rates, dollar, risk aversion |
| Typical economic drivers | USD strength, oil (India is a large net crude importer), risk appetite, rate differentials, flows | Global activity, inventories, spare capacity, USD | Real yields, USD, safe-haven demand |
| Sign prior for $\Omega$ on $r_{\text{USDINR}}$ | $+$ for broad USD, $+$ for Brent (INR weakens when oil rises), $-$ for risk-on equities | — | — |
| Data trap | Spot vs fixing vs NDF; trading-hour asynchrony (§5.5) | Futures-roll gaps and term-structure ([Gorton & Rouwenhorst 2006](https://doi.org/10.2469/faj.v62.n2.4083)); use a roll-adjusted continuous series, not a naive splice | Spot vs futures basis; fix vs close |
| Mean predictability evidence | Weak ([Rossi 2013](https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.51.4.1063)) | Short-horizon gains over no-change are possible with fundamentals/VARs at monthly horizons, but daily gains are not robust ([Baumeister & Kilian 2015](https://ideas.repec.org:443/p/cpr/ceprdp/9569.html) ✅; [Alquist & Kilian 2010](https://doi.org/10.1002/jae.1159)) | Near random walk at daily frequency; hedge/safe-haven properties are conditional ([Baur & Lucey 2010](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6288.2010.00244.x); [Baur & McDermott 2010](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbankfin.2009.12.008); survey [O'Connor et al. 2015](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.irfa.2015.07.005); [Erb & Harvey 2013](https://doi.org/10.2469/faj.v69.n4.1)) |
| Variance features | Moderate persistence, intervention-induced jumps | High volatility, regime clusters (supply shocks) | Lower volatility, high persistence, spikes in crisis |
| Expected $\hat\nu$ | Moderate | Low (heavy tails) | Moderate |
| Financialization/cross-asset | Linked to oil and USD | Linked to USD and risk sentiment ([Tang & Xiong 2012](https://doi.org/10.2469/faj.v68.n5.6)) | Linked to USD and real rates |

*Rows describing expected parameter magnitudes are qualitative priors from the literature; they are **not** results of this pipeline.*

### 5.5 Time-zone asynchrony (important for any lead–lag interpretation)

The script correctly states that a surviving $\Omega_j$ is a *lead–lag association*, not structural causation. A concrete mechanism that can manufacture lead–lag without causation is **non-synchronous closes**: Asian equity closes precede, and U.S. equity/volatility closes follow, an Indian-session FX observation. A U.S. return dated $t$ can then be genuinely earlier than, or overlap with, part of the next FX return $r_{t+1}$. The construction is *causal* in the information sense (no regressor post-dates its target) — verified in §8 — but the economic reading of "oil leads INR" should be tempered by the sampling times of each series. For Gold/Brent (near-24h markets) the same issue arises with fixing/settlement times.

### 5.6 Transformations of drives

The drive columns are daily *log returns*. For the VIX and the U.S. 10-year yield this is a transformation of a *level* that is a ratio or a rate: log-changes of yields are not equal to basis-point changes and become distorted at low yield levels. The choice is internally coherent (all drives are in additive return units and are standardized), but the economic interpretation of $\Omega_{\text{us10y}}$ is "per sd of log-yield change". Likewise `broad_usd` includes INR with a small weight, producing mild endogeneity when the target is USD/INR; this should be kept in mind when interpreting its coefficient.

### 5.7 Cumulative-return forecasting and horizon

At $H>1$ the mean skill (if any) is overshadowed by variance growth ($\propto H$) while overlapping targets reduce the effective sample size by roughly $H$. The script's multi-horizon design (direct regression with purged training; CRPS with spaced origins and block bootstrap) is the right architecture for this.

