## 6. Statistical analysis of the evaluation design

### 6.1 Out-of-sample $R^2$ and nested-model testing

$R^2_{\text{oos}}=1-\mathrm{SSE}_{\text{model}}/\mathrm{SSE}_{\text{zero}}$. Against a zero forecast this is the Campbell–Thompson-type statistic ([2008](https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhm055)). Because the candidate contains the zero forecast as a special case, plain MSPE comparison is biased against the larger model; the Clark–West adjustment fixes this:

$$
f_t=(y-b)^2-\big[(y-a)^2-(b-a)^2\big]=2\,(y-b)(a-b),
$$

(algebra verified in §8) tested one-sided with Newey–West HAC variance ([Clark & West 2007](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeconom.2006.05.023); [Newey & West 1987](https://doi.org/10.2307/1913610)). Related: [Diebold & Mariano 1995](https://doi.org/10.1080/07350015.1995.10524599), [West 1996](https://doi.org/10.2307/2171864), [Giacomini & White 2006](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-0262.2006.00718.x).

**Validity qualifications (limitations, not errors).**
* CW's "approximately normal" result is derived for OLS-estimated nested linear models; here the alternative is a *penalized, weighted, CV-tuned* estimator and the sample is *expanding*. Normal critical values are then approximate; [Rogoff & Stavrakeva (2008)](https://www.nber.org/papers/w14071) ✅ caution against over-reliance on asymptotic CW statistics. Bootstrap/window-robust checks ([Rossi & Inoue 2012](https://doi.org/10.1080/07350015.2012.663261)) are the remedy.
* For $H>1$, the script uses HAC lag $H-1$, the standard choice for MA($H-1$) overlap, with the usual finite-sample caveat.

### 6.2 Power

Approximating the CW $t$-statistic by $t\approx\sqrt{nR^2_{\text{pop}}/(1-R^2_{\text{pop}})}$:

| True $R^2$ | $n=1000$ | one-sided 5% power (Gaussian approx.) |
|---|---|---|
| 0.5% | $t\approx2.24$ | $\approx73\%$ |
| 0.1% | $t\approx1.00$ | $\approx26\%$ |

This supports the code's comment that $\sim$1000 OOS days are needed to detect $R^2\approx0.5\%$, and shows that smaller effects are essentially undetectable at daily frequency with a few years of data.

### 6.3 Density evaluation

* **PIT** $u_t=F_{t}(y_t)$ is i.i.d. $U(0,1)$ under a correctly specified one-step density ([Rosenblatt 1952]; [Diebold, Gunther & Tay 1998](https://doi.org/10.2307/2527342)). The code computes $u_t=F_{t_\nu}(z_t/\sqrt{(\nu-2)/\nu})$, consistent with the standardization.
* **Log score** is strictly proper ([Gneiting & Raftery 2007](https://doi.org/10.1198/016214506000001437)). The code uses $\log f_{t_\nu}(z/ts)-\log ts-\tfrac12\log h_t$ with $ts=\sqrt{(\nu-2)/\nu}$ — correct Jacobian — and the benchmark $\log\mathcal N(x;0,s_0^2)$ in the same scaled units, so the *difference* is unit-free.
* **KS test on PIT** treats PIT as i.i.d.; estimation error in frozen parameters and residual dependence make the p-value approximate. [Berkowitz (2001)](https://doi.org/10.1198/073500101316970395) tests are the more powerful alternative.
* **Tail coverage** ($\Pr[u<0.05]$, $\Pr[u>0.95]$) is the unconditional part of [Christoffersen (1998)](https://doi.org/10.2307/2527341); a conditional-coverage (independence) test is not implemented.
* **Diagnostics:** Ljung–Box ([1978](https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/65.2.297)) on $z$ (mean dependence), ARCH-LM ([Engle 1982](https://doi.org/10.2307/1912773)) on $z$ (volatility dependence), OLS-CUSUM on demeaned $z$ ([Brown, Durbin & Evans 1975](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1975.tb01532.x); [Ploberger & Krämer 1992](https://doi.org/10.2307/2951597)).
* **Stationarity check.** The ADF test ([Dickey & Fuller 1979](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1979.10482531)) rejects a unit root; it does not by itself verify constancy of the variance. The script's wording ("mean and variance of $r$ do not drift") is slightly broader than what ADF establishes; the GARCH persistence report and the CUSUM/ARCH diagnostics supply the missing evidence.

### 6.4 CRPS

$\mathrm{CRPS}(F,y)=\mathbb E|X-y|-\tfrac12\mathbb E|X-X'|$ is strictly proper ([Matheson & Winkler 1976](https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.22.10.1087); [Gneiting & Raftery 2007](https://doi.org/10.1198/016214506000001437)). For sorted ensemble members, $\sum_{i,j}|x_i-x_j|=2\sum_i(2i-n-1)x_{(i)}$, so $\tfrac12\mathbb E|X-X'|$ is $\sum_i(2i-n-1)x_{(i)}/n^2$ — exactly the code's expression (the V-statistic). Using the "fair" $n(n-1)$ denominator ([Ferro 2014](https://doi.org/10.1002/qj.2270)) changes all models' scores by the same small factor because every ensemble has the same $n$; ranking and differences are unaffected to first order. See also [Hersbach 2000](https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0434(2000)015%3C0559:DOTCRP%3E2.0.CO;2).

Moving-block bootstrap for score differences ([Künsch 1989](https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176347265); block-length guidance [Politis & White 2004](https://doi.org/10.1081/ETC-120028836)) is appropriate; with $H\le21$ and origin stride 20 the chosen block length ($\max(2,\lceil H/\text{stride}\rceil)=2$) matches the weak overlap.

### 6.5 Leakage audit (all verified)

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Features at row $t$ use only information through day $t$ | ✔ (mode states are causal filters; drives aligned by cumulative-sum differencing) |
| Target row $t$ is $r_{t+1}$ | ✔ (`shift(-1)`) |
| Walk-forward training stops strictly before the test block | ✔ (`X.iloc[:start+s]`) |
| Expanding-mean benchmark excludes the contemporaneous target | ✔ (`shift(1)` of the expanding mean of $y$ gives $\bar y_{0..t-1}=\overline{r_{1..t}}$) |
| Multi-horizon purging | ✔ (`train_end = first_test_pos − H + 1`; row $\text{pos}-H$ has its target mature at $\text{pos}$, known at the origin) |
| Density parameters frozen on training data; variance filtered with past OOS errors only | ✔ |
| CRPS origins leave $H$ future observations; fits use `feats.iloc[:origin+1]` only | ✔ |
| Hyper-parameters re-selected inside every walk-forward refit | ✔ |

### 6.6 Researcher degrees of freedom

The suite contains several correlated variants (penalty multipliers $\{1,0.3,0.1,0.03,0.01\}$, horizons 1/5/21, several tests). Selecting the best of these on the same outcomes inflates apparent skill ([White 2000](https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00152); [Hansen 2005](https://doi.org/10.1198/073500105000000063); [Harvey, Liu & Zhu 2016](https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhv059); [Bailey, Borwein, López de Prado & Zhu 2017](https://doi.org/10.21314/JCF.2016.322)). The **nested chronological validation** and the **frozen forward evaluation** are exactly the right countermeasures: the selection period fixes the multiplier, the later hold-out or post-cutoff window scores it once. They are valid *only if* the cutoff and multiplier are fixed before examining post-cutoff outcomes, which the script states; with fewer than 20 origins the script correctly labels results preliminary. Note the default `--frozen-cutoff 2026-10-16` leaves, as of 2026-10-30, at most a handful of 5-day non-overlapping origins; the output will be preliminary unless the data are refreshed and the test is re-run later.

