## 7. Computational and data analysis

### 7.1 Data alignment (the central data-engineering step)

Per drive: cumulative sum of log-returns on its own calendar $\to$ forward-fill onto the INR calendar $\to$ first difference. Because log-returns add, this yields the *exact* drive return over $(t_{\text{prev}},t]$: moves on days when the INR calendar is closed are carried into the next INR day; a drive that is closed on an INR day contributes 0. Missing driver values are treated as zero contributions and counted in `DATA_QUALITY`; the run aborts if any drive is missing on more than 10% of INR dates. The design is sound and the policy is disclosed. Limitation: "missing" and "holiday" are not distinguished (§9).

### 7.2 Complexity

| Stage | Cost (approx.) |
|---|---|
| Mode states | $O(n|\mathcal H|)$ via `lfilter` |
| ElasticNetCV | $O\big(|\rho\text{-grid}|\cdot N_\alpha\cdot K_{\text{folds}}\cdot\text{iters}\cdot n p\big)$ with $p=|\mathcal H|+|\text{drives}|=12$ |
| GARCH-$t$ | iterative ML, $O(n)$ per likelihood evaluation |
| Simulation | $O(N\cdot H\cdot p)$ plus one vectorized `predict` per day |
| Walk-forward | (test window / refit interval) × (CV + GARCH) |
| CRPS | $|\text{horizons}|\times|\text{origins}|\times2$ fits + simulations; $O(n\log n)$ CRPS per ensemble |

Memory is dominated by $N\times p$ arrays: trivial at $N=20{,}000$.

### 7.3 Numerical conditioning

The ×1000 scaling brings residuals to order 1–10 for the `arch` optimizer; because the scaling is a pure change of units, estimates are equivariant. The 1e-12 variance floor in the CRPS simulator protects against underflow; the main simulator relies on $\omega>0$.

### 7.4 Reproducibility

Seeded `numpy.random.default_rng` (PCG64) is used throughout; ensembles for different origins/models use deterministic seed offsets. Library versions should be pinned (scikit-learn $\ge1.7$ per the header). Software: [scikit-learn (Pedregosa et al. 2011)](https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/pedregosa11a.html), [statsmodels (Seabold & Perktold 2010)](https://doi.org/10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-011), [SciPy (Virtanen et al. 2020)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2), [NumPy (Harris et al. 2020)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2), [`arch` (Sheppard)](https://github.com/bashtage/arch).

### 7.5 Multi-asset dashboard

* USD/INR: this GLE/VARX pipeline run as a subprocess (`--skip-eval`, 500 paths).
* Gold, Brent: independent **moving-block bootstrap** (block 5) of each asset's own historical log-returns, $P_h=P_T\exp(\sum_{i\le h}r^{*}_i)$ — a *scenario baseline* with no mean model and implicitly the sample drift.
* Cross-asset analysis: Pearson correlation of same-date log-returns and the latest rolling-60D correlation (≥40 common points). Descriptive only; **the three forecasts are simulated independently**, so the dashboard does not impose these correlations on the forecasts.
* The thin paths and the terminal histogram in the browser are **regenerated from the stored P05/P50/P95** using smoothed Gaussian noise (marginal sd $=(p_{95}-p_{05})/3.29$); the page states this. The terminal histogram is therefore a *Gaussian-shaped visual approximation*, not the model's terminal distribution (which is skewed in price space and fat-tailed in return space).

